==English==
{{wp}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈleɪtɪv/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪtɪv|s=3}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*telh₂- (bear)}}
From {{uder|en|la|ēlātus||exalted, lofty, high}} {{suffix|en||ive}}. Possibly borrowed from {{bor|en|de|-}}, which has produced a great deal of pioneering Semitist literature, and where {{m|de|Elativ}} is a common term for {{l|en|absolute superlative}} in all languages.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{c|en|Semitic linguistics}} In Semitic languages, the “adjective degree of superiority.” In some languages such as [[Arabic]], the concepts of [[comparative]] and [[superlative]] degree of an adjective are merged into a single form, the '''elative'''. How this form is understood or translated depends upon context and definiteness. In the absence of comparison, the elative conveys the notion of “[[greatest]]”, “[[supreme]].”
#: ''The elative of {{m|ar|كَبِير||big}} is {{m|ar|أَكْبَر||bigger/biggest, greater/greatest}}.''

