====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|rare}} [[exalted|Exalted]]; feeling [[elation]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Thomas Lodge|Thomas Lodge]]|title=A fig for Momus|year=1595||passage=And so the fleas, and flies in their degree, / By their attracted moyst humiditie, / Drawne from a certaine vertue '''elatiue''', / Whence raine his generation doth deriue: / Seeke more than their accustom'd nutriment.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James Struthers|year=1838|title=Poetic Tales|page=125|passage=Thither shall gratitude's feelings '''elative''' wend, / Bath'd in the dew of the soul's lofty swelling.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=May 1910|journal=State Hospital Bullitens|publisher=[New York] State Commission in Lunacy|volume=III|number=1|title=The Diagnosis of Brain Syphilis|author=David Henderson|passage=The first case is that of E. M., 37 years of age, laborer, who on admission was '''elative''', over-talkative, had a well marked feeling of well being.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1984|author=Richard Taylor|title=Good and Evil: A New Direction: a Forceful Attack on the Rationalistic Tradition in Ethics|page=242|passage=whereas if one thinks instead that it is inflicted by others, then the '''elative''' feeling is one of masochism, which is quite rightly deemed a disease.}}

===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*telh₂- (bear)}}
{{rfv-etym|en}} From {{uder|en|la|ēlātus|}}, perfect past participle of {{m|la|efferō||I carry out or away}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|grammar}} In Finno-Ugric languages, one of the [[locative case]]s, expressing “out of,” or “from” as in Finnish [[talo]]sta, Hungarian [[házból]] (“out of the [[house]]”). Its opposite is the [[illative case]] (“into”). In Finnish, the case form is used also to express "out of" or "proximity" in a figurative sense which in English is often conveyed by the word '''"[[about]]"'''.

